DNA carries a history, yes. Immutable is where the blockchain analogy bends. You inherit DNA from earlier generations, and some differences persist long enough to help reconstruct ancestry. But DNA can mutate, and recombination can bring stretches from different ancestors together. A genome gives you surviving traces of the past. It rarely tells you exactly when each change happened or what came before it.

If you mean the River Severn landscape, picture a bend in the river. The present channel is what you can see. Old channels and layers of sediment can give you clues about where the river used to run, and later floods can erase some of those clues. A blockchain aims to preserve an ordered record of entries. A genome is closer to a landscape still being reshaped by the processes that left its history there.

That gives Previously, in a Different Life a better mystery. Everyone assumes the family’s history is written in the blood. Then two siblings inherit different pieces of the same past, and it takes both of them to see where the river used to run.